# Chroma library, modern Earth: sexual orientation and gender identity (next batch, Library thread, 2026-10-05).
# Emren's point 11 and the card "Sex set, rest found"; Emren 21:00: "society and peer-pressure, events and histories must
# be important for sex orientation and experience impact". Research and rules: drafts/identity-research.md.
# Sex is set at setup. Attraction (attr 0 to 4) and gender unease are hidden traits drawn at real rates and changed by no
# act; these moments are how they are found in play, and how the society around the character carries them.
# Gates for the engine are the "# gate:" lines above each situation. No moment presents a past event as a cause, no
# pressure to change works, nothing sexual involves anyone under 18, and nothing is graphic.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: library-spec.md and drafts/next2/BRIEF.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- inner: a first stirring (cycle s1)

# gate: attr >= 1 & age >= 10
== a feeling for a friend that does not fit
tier: inner
stages: child juvenile
age: 10-15
tone: mixed
life: inner, friends, love
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, sibling
earth: a warm, confusing feeling for a friend of your own sex, not the crush everyone expects you to have
requires: drawn at least a little to the same sex (attr >= 1) and aged 10 or more; a close friend of the same sex seen most days (school, a team, a club), in the months when the first crushes start in the class
likelier: puberty and the first couples in the class (median age of a first such thought is 12, Pew 2013); a close same-sex friendship; a film, a song or a person in the news that shows such feelings; a time and place where they can be named
rarer: no close friend of the same sex; a home or a school where such feelings are never mentioned (they come all the same, and are named later)
times: Once, around 10 to 14, for most young people drawn at all to their own sex; Pew 2013: LGB adults first thought they might be at a median age of 12
once: yes
scene: {friend} leans over on the bus to share an earphone, and {Ns} stomach turns over. Everyone in the class has started talking about crushes, and this feels like what they describe, only not about the person anyone would expect.
scene W: Everyone knows how it is meant to go: the class pairs off, boys with girls, and the grown-ups tease. {N} has always kept the rules, and this feeling is not in them.
scene U: {N} notices it the way one notices a new word: a warmth when {friend} sits close, a sting when {friend} talks about someone else. {N} wants to know what it is called.
scene B: {N} works out quickly that this is a feeling to keep close. Whatever it is, it belongs to {N}, and nobody is going to make gossip of it.
scene R: When {friend} grins, {Ns} heart bangs like a door in the wind. It is the best feeling {N} has ever had, and the most confusing.
scene G: Something new is growing in {N}, quiet as a bulb under snow. It is not what anyone said would come, but it feels like part of {N} all the same.
- W | tell yourself it is only a close friendship, and carry on as normal | habit | v: conformity, security | chance: 85
- U | look the feeling up quietly, page after page, until it has a name | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B | keep it entirely to yourself, and notice who could be trusted with it one day | v: security | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 85
- R | write your friend a note about the feeling, and slip it into their bag | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 62
- G | let the feeling be, without deciding yet what it means | v: self-direction (accepting oneself) | chance: 85
- W>U | ask a trusted adult, in general terms, whether such feelings are normal | door | v: security, self-direction | chance: 74
- U>B | keep a private diary in a code only you can read | habit | v: security, self-direction | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 80
- B>R | arrange to sit next to your friend in every class you can | v: hedonism | chance: 75
- R>G | spend every free afternoon outdoors with your friend, glad just to be near | habit | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 95
- G>W | keep the friendship just as it is, so nothing spoils the old group | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 75
works: The feeling has a shape now, and {N} carries it more easily.
works: {N} and {friend} stay close, and the strange new feeling becomes part of the year.
fails: {N} lies awake for weeks, more muddled than before.
fails: Something shifts with {friend}, and the easy closeness of before is gone.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- inner: gender unease, the clothes and the name (cycle s2)

# gate: unease & age >= 8
== the clothes and the name that do not fit
tier: inner
stages: child juvenile young_adult
age: 8-25
tone: trouble
life: inner, body, family
horizon: months
roles: parent, grandparent, sibling, friend
earth: the clothes, the name and the place you are given as a boy or a girl feel like someone else's, or only half yours
requires: gender unease (unease: the sex they were raised as does not fit their own gender, or not only; trans or nonbinary) and aged 8 or more; a day that sorts boys from girls (a uniform, a team, a party outfit, the name called out)
likelier: the years just before and during puberty (about 1.4 in 100 of 13 to 17 year-olds identify as transgender, Williams Institute 2022); a school or a family that splits boys and girls sharply; a family occasion with clothes laid out; a book, a film or a person who shows another way to be
rarer: a family and a school with loose ideas of how boys and girls dress and play (the feeling is the same; it pinches less)
times: Most young people with gender unease, again and again from childhood into the twenties; about 1.4 in 100 of 13 to 17 year-olds identify as transgender (Williams Institute 2022)
gap: 3-6
scene: It is {grandparent}'s birthday, and {N} has been dressed for the photographs the way everyone expects. In the hall mirror the clothes look like a costume, and when {parent} calls {Ns} name, it sounds like someone else's.
scene W: {N} knows what is expected at a family party and does it, every button done up. In the photographs {N} stands up straight and feels like a stand-in for somebody else.
scene U: {N} has been keeping notes for years, in a way: which clothes feel like a disguise, which games feel like home, which name {N} answers to in daydreams.
scene B: {N} has learned to wear the clothes like armour and give nothing away. Nobody needs to know what goes on underneath, not yet.
scene R: {N} wants to rip off the stiff collar and run. Everything in the mirror is wrong, or only half right, and the wrongness burns.
scene G: {N} feels like a plant in the wrong pot: the roots are fine, but they press against the sides.
- W | wear what is expected today, and keep the peace for the family | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 88
- U | read everything you can find about people who have felt this way | door | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B | keep a few clothes of your own choosing hidden, for when you are alone | habit | v: self-direction | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 80
- R | change into clothes that feel right the moment the photographs are done | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: breaking the family's dress code at a party; backfire: a row in front of the guests, and sent upstairs | norm: transition | chance: 90
- G | tell yourself this is how you are made, and wait until it can show | v: self-direction (accepting oneself) | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 75
- W>B | offer to keep every house rule for a month, in return for choosing your own clothes | self_control: + | v: self-direction | chance: 62
- U>R | change your style quietly, one piece at a time, so nobody makes a fuss | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 65
- B>G | find the one relative who will not mind, and spend the party at their side | v: security, benevolence | chance: 70
- R>W | blurt out to your sibling that the name feels wrong, and ask them to understand | v: self-direction, benevolence | mark: named their gender | chance: 62
- G>U | slip out into the garden alone, and listen to what the feeling is saying | v: self-direction | closed: approval: slipping away from a family party; backfire: missed for the photographs, and a scolding | chance: 75
works: {N} gets through the day with something of {Ns} own still whole.
works: For a few hours the feeling eases, and {N} catches a glimpse of who {N} might be.
fails: The day is long and the photographs are worse, and {N} cannot bear to look at them.
fails: There is a row, and the wrongness is sharper afterwards.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday pair 1 (no gate; juvenile to mature): calls UBR (cycle s3) / calls WG (cycle s2)

== someone mocked for being different
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 11-67
calls: UBR
tone: trouble
life: school, work, friends
horizon: week
roles: rival, friend, colleague, a teacher or a manager
earth: a classmate or a colleague is mocked by the group for not acting the way a boy or a girl, a man or a woman, is supposed to
times: Most teenagers see it many times at school, adults now and then at work; GLSEN surveys find nearly all LGBTQ pupils in US schools hear such remarks (adult part an estimate)
gap: 3-8
scene: In the corridor {rival} and two others trail a quiet member of the group, calling out the word they have used all month, and half the corridor laughs.
scene W: The rules on the noticeboard say nobody is to be picked on, and here it is happening right under them. {N} feels the duty to say something like a weight on the chest.
scene U: {N} has watched it for weeks and seen the pattern: always the same three, always when nobody in charge is near, always the same word.
scene B: {N} sees how it works: whoever stands next to the target becomes the next one. Keeping clear is the smart thing, and {N} hates how smart it is.
scene R: The laughter makes {Ns} face burn. {N} wants to shove through the crowd and tell {rival} where to go.
scene G: The one being mocked walks with eyes on the floor, the way a hurt animal moves. {N} remembers them on the first day, shy and hopeful, before everyone sorted themselves into groups.
- W | report the name-calling to whoever is in charge, with the dates and the names | v: universalism, conformity | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 70
- U | ask them, away from everyone, what would actually help | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 80
- B | keep your distance, so the mocking does not turn on you | v: security | mark: refused someone in need | act: keep their distance, so the mocking does not turn on them | chance: 88
- R | step in front of them and tell the mockers to back off | identity | v: universalism, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | chance: 75
- G | sit with them at lunch every day, as if nothing were odd | habit | identity | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 82
- W>R | ask whoever is in charge for a group talk where everyone can speak freely | door | v: universalism | chance: 80
- U>G | find out what they love, and bring them along to a club where it fits | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 70
- B>W | warn the ringleader that a video exists and will reach the people in charge | v: power, universalism | closed: approval: threats; backfire: the video goes round, and the one being mocked is hurt again | chance: 62
- R>U | ask the mockers, loudly and in front of everyone, why it bothers them so much | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 75
- G>B | laugh along with the rest, since that is how the pecking order works | self_control: - | v: power, security | mark: gave in to pressure | closed: approval: joining in the bullying; backfire: someone in charge hears, and there is trouble | chance: 68
works: By the end of the week the corridor is quieter, and {N} can live with the part played in it.
works: Things settle, and within a month the one being mocked walks a little taller.
fails: The mocking goes on, and {N} feels sick about it every morning.
fails: The mockers turn on {N} as well, and the next weeks are hard ones.

== your sibling brings home a same-sex partner
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 12-75
calls: WG
tone: mixed
life: family
horizon: moment
roles: sibling, parent, grandparent
earth: at the holiday meal a brother, a sister or a cousin brings home a partner of the same sex for the first time
times: About 1 in 4 people, once (estimate); about 9 in 100 US adults identify as LGBTQ+ (Gallup 2024), so many families have a brother, sister or cousin who will
once: yes
share: .25
scene: {sibling} arrives for the holiday meal and, for the first time, brings a partner: someone of the same sex, holding flowers and a cake, smiling hard. Around the table the family goes very polite.
scene W: {N} was raised to welcome every guest at this table, and also on teachings that have hard words for this. Both are being tested before the soup.
scene U: {N} watches the room: {parent}'s fixed smile, {grandparent}'s silence, the partner trying too hard. It is like a play where nobody has learned their lines.
scene B: {N} sees at once that all the family's attention will be on {sibling} today. The usual questions about {Ns} own life will not come up, and that is not nothing.
scene R: {N} sees the partner's hands shaking round the cake tin and wants to do something, anything, to break the ice.
scene G: The family has gathered round this table for every holiday {N} can remember. Today it has one more chair, and the old table holds it.
- W | lay a place for the partner, and treat them as any other guest | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 90
- U | ask the partner about their work and their life, and really listen | v: universalism | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- B | stay polite but distant, and keep your opinions to yourself | v: security | chance: 92
- R | raise a glass to the two of them before anyone can make it awkward | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 80
- G | sit beside your grandparent and help them come round, slowly | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 62
- W>B | refuse to sit at the same table, and let the family choose | identity | v: tradition, power | closed: approval: snubbing a guest at the family table; backfire: the family chooses, and not as hoped | norm: -same-sex marriage | chance: 60
- U>R | start a quiz after dinner, so everyone has to talk and laugh together | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 75
- B>G | take your parent aside: accept this, or lose your sibling for good | v: benevolence, power | chance: 58
- R>W | call out the uncle who makes a snide remark, right there at the table | v: universalism | chance: 70
- G>U | take the partner round the garden after lunch, and get to know them | door | v: benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 88
works: By the pudding the talk is easy, and the partner is laughing at the old family jokes.
works: {sibling} catches {Ns} eye across the table and mouths a silent thanks.
fails: The meal limps to an early end, and {sibling} and the partner leave before the coffee.
fails: Something said at the table is not forgotten, and the next holiday is thinner.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- inner: a dawning certainty (cycle s3)

# gate: attr >= 2 & age >= 13
== the feeling is not going to pass
tier: inner
stages: juvenile young_adult
age: 13-22
tone: mixed
life: inner, love
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, sibling
earth: one ordinary evening it becomes clear that the pull toward your own sex is not a phase and is not going away
requires: drawn to both sexes or mostly the same sex (attr >= 2) and aged 13 or more; a first stirring some years before, and the feeling has outlasted crushes, teasing and school years
likelier: the mid to late teens (median age of feeling sure is 17, Pew 2013); a friend, a film or a public figure that names it; a time and place where it can be said; a first crush that is returned
rarer: a home, a faith or a country where it is condemned (the certainty comes later, often in the twenties; it is not prevented)
times: Once, in the mid to late teens, for most people drawn to their own sex, later where it is condemned; Pew 2013: LGB adults felt sure at a median age of 17
once: yes
scene: {N} stands by the wall at the school dance, watching the couples sway, and the thought arrives whole and quiet: this is not a phase. It has not passed in four years, and it is not going to.
scene W: {N} has waited years for the feeling to fade, as everyone said it would. Tonight {N} knows it will not, and every plan the family has made for {N} wobbles.
scene U: {N} has tested it like a theory, year after year, and the results always come out the same. There is no mistake in the sums.
scene B: So this is how it is. {N} turns the fact over and starts working out who must never know, who might, and what it will cost.
scene R: The certainty arrives like a door flung open. {N} is scared stiff and, underneath, wildly happy.
scene G: It feels less like a discovery than a recognition, like hearing a song whose words {N} has always known.
- W | decide to keep it quiet until you have left home | v: security, conformity | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 85
- U | find honest books and sites, and read until the fear has answers | v: self-direction | chance: 85
- B | promise yourself to live on your own terms, whoever minds | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- R | say it out loud in your empty room, just to hear how it sounds | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 92
- G | look for a group in town where young people like this meet | door | v: security (belonging) | mark: made a friend | chance: 72
- W>R | make a plan for the day you are free to be yourself, and stick to it | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- U>G | learn the long history of people who felt the same, until it feels ordinary | v: universalism | chance: 75
- B>W | decide what to show and what to hide, person by person, to keep the peace | v: security | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 80
- R>U | pour every feeling onto paper in one honest, furious night | v: self-direction | chance: 68
- G>B | keep to the family's ways for now, and let nobody guess | v: tradition, security | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 88
works: The certainty settles into something {N} can live with.
works: {N} wakes the next morning feeling lighter, and more like {N}.
fails: Fear crowds in for weeks, and {N} sleeps badly.
fails: {N} pushes the thought away, and it comes back louder.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday pair 2 (attr >= 2; juvenile to adult): calls UR (cycle s1) / calls WBG (cycle s2)

# gate: attr >= 2 & ~mark('came out') & age >= 14
== the first person you tell
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 14-45
calls: UR
tone: mixed
life: friends, love
horizon: moment
roles: friend, sibling
earth: after years of knowing, you tell one person, a close friend or a sibling, that you are gay or bisexual
times: Most gay and bisexual people, once; Pew 2013: LGB adults first told a close friend or family member at a median age of about 20, younger generations sooner
gap: 2-4
scene: {N} and {friend} are sitting on the low wall outside the chip shop, as they have a hundred times, and {N} has decided that tonight is the night. The words are ready. The mouth is dry.
scene W: {N} has chosen {friend} with care: someone who keeps promises. {N} only hopes that {friend}'s ideas of right and wrong have room for this.
scene U: {N} has rehearsed it a dozen ways and guessed {friend}'s answer to each. None of the guesses help now.
scene B: {N} has weighed it up: {friend} is loyal, has secrets too, and has more to lose by telling than by keeping quiet. It is as safe a bet as {N} will get.
scene R: {Ns} heart is going like a drum. Either it comes out now or it stays in for good, and {N} cannot stand for good.
scene G: {N} has known {friend} since they were both small. If anyone in the world can take this as part of the same old {N}, it is {friend}.
- W | ask your friend to promise to keep it secret, then tell them | v: security | mark: came out | chance: 80
- U | test the water first with a question about someone famous who came out | v: security | chance: 82
- B | tell only the friend with a secret of their own to keep | v: security, power | mark: came out | chance: 82
- R | just say it, straight out, before the nerve goes | v: self-direction | mark: came out | chance: 75
- G | wait for a quiet moment that feels right, and let it come out naturally | identity | v: self-direction | mark: came out | chance: 78
- W>U | write it in a careful letter, so the words come out right | v: self-direction | mark: came out | chance: 80
- U>B | say it is about someone else, and watch how your friend reacts | self_control: - | v: security | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 78
- B>R | wait for a night when your friend owes you one, then pour it all out | v: self-direction | mark: came out | act: wait for a night when the friend owes them one, then pour it all out | chance: 78
- R>G | blurt it out in tears, and let your friend hold you | v: benevolence, security | mark: came out | grants: friend for life | act: blurt it out in tears, and let the friend hold them | chance: 85
- G>W | decide not to tell anyone yet, and keep the peace a while longer | v: security, conformity | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 82
works: {friend} listens to the end, and then says the one thing {N} needed to hear.
works: The night goes on and the world has not ended; if anything it is bigger.
fails: {friend} goes stiff and changes the subject, and the walk home is very quiet.
fails: Within a week someone else knows, and {N} did not tell them.

# gate: attr >= 2 & age >= 15
== a love nobody can know about
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 15-40
calls: WBG
tone: mixed
life: love
horizon: months
roles: prospect, friend, parent
earth: a first love with someone of your own sex, and nobody can know: not the family, not the school or the work, not the street
times: Many gay and bisexual people, once, in the teens or twenties, more often where it is condemned (estimate); hiding adds lasting stress (Meyer 2003)
once: yes
scene: {N} and {prospect} meet at the bus shelter at the far end of the park, where nobody they know ever goes. They hold hands for twenty minutes and let go the moment a car turns the corner.
scene W: {N} hates the lying: the invented evening class, the friend's house that is really the park. Honesty was always the first rule, and love has made {N} break it every week.
scene U: {N} has built a whole system: separate chats, a nickname in the phone, routes home that never cross. It works, and it is exhausting.
scene B: Nobody gets to take this. {N} guards the secret like money, and enjoys a little the power of a life nobody else knows about.
scene R: It is the best thing that has ever happened, and {N} wants to shout it from the rooftops, and cannot.
scene G: The love feels as natural as breathing. It is the hiding that feels unnatural, like a plant kept in a cupboard.
- W | keep it secret for now, but refuse to tell any outright lies | v: conformity, security | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 80
- U | plan every meeting with care, so nobody ever finds out | v: security | mark: kept it hidden | closed: approval: sneaking out behind the family's back; backfire: caught, and the questions start | chance: 80
- B | let people think you are seeing someone else, as a cover | v: security | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 78
- R | walk down the high street hand in hand, and let people see | identity | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: came out | closed: approval: a same-sex couple where people frown on it; backfire: the talk of the street, and trouble at home | norm: coming out | chance: 65
- G | bring them home as your best friend, and let the family grow fond of them | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 78
- W>B | get a friend to cover for the meetings, with a story agreed in advance | v: security | closed: approval: deceiving the family; backfire: the friend slips, and both stories fall apart | chance: 75
- U>R | plan a day trip to the city, where two people can hold hands in the street | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 75
- B>G | lie about weekend shifts, and spend the hours together by the river | v: hedonism | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 77
- R>W | swear to each other to stop hiding by the summer, whatever it costs | binds | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 90
- G>U | take it slowly, and learn who the other person really is | self_control: + | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 77
works: The months together are the happiest {N} has known, secret or not.
works: The two of them find a small, safe corner of the world that is only theirs.
fails: Someone sees them, and by Monday the story is everywhere.
fails: The strain of the hiding is too much, and {prospect} ends it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: telling the family (pairs D5b)

# gate: (attr >= 2 | unease) & age >= 13
== telling the family who you are
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 13-65
per_year: 0 .025 .035 .02 .01 0
drivers: era+.5 ties+.2 prosper+.1 harsh-.4 unrest-.2 stress-.1
once: yes
times: once, if ever: per person it concerns (attr >= 2, or gender unease) about 2 to 4 in 100 a year from the mid-teens into the thirties and fewer later, so that about two in three tell the family in a lifetime (estimate from Pew 2013: median first telling at about 20; about 6 in 10 LGB adults have told their mother and 4 in 10 their father, bisexual people far less often); younger generations tell sooner. Closes at 65, when most parents are gone; later tellings come through 'a late truth inside a marriage' and 'a name that has waited for decades'
likelier: a time and place where it is legal and spoken of; a family that has said kind things about people like this; a partner to bring home; a steady income or a home of one's own; friends who already know
rarer: a family faith or a community that condemns it; a country that punishes it; depending on the family for money or a roof; a family already in crisis
tone: mixed
life: family, identity
horizon: years
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent
earth: you tell your parents who you love, or who you are, and wait for what comes next
scene: Sunday lunch is over and {parent} is stacking the plates when {N} asks everyone to sit back down. The sentence has been rehearsed for years, and now it comes out in one breath.
scene W: {N} wants to do this properly: at home, at the table, with the whole family, the way important things have always been said. {N} only hopes the family's rules have room for the truth.
scene U: {N} has thought it all through: who to tell first, the questions that will come, the leaflet folded in a pocket for {parent}. Nothing on paper says how a face will look.
scene B: {N} has chosen the moment with care: after the job offer, with a place of {Ns} own lined up. Whatever the family says, nobody can take away the roof.
scene R: {N} is done with pretending at every birthday and every holiday. It is coming out today, whatever happens, and {Ns} hands are shaking.
scene G: {N} wants the family to know the whole of {N}, the way they have always known each other's joys and troubles. Belonging to them by halves is no longer enough.
- W | tell them all together at the family table, calmly and with respect | identity | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: came out | chance: 60
- U | tell them with answers ready, and meet every question with patience | identity | v: universalism | mark: came out | chance: 64
- B | wait until you no longer depend on them, then tell them on your own terms | identity | self_control: + | v: self-direction, security | mark: came out | chance: 62
- R | say it at the big family party, out loud, and let the chips fall | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: came out | chance: 60
- G | tell your grandparent first, and let the news travel through the family gently | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: came out | chance: 60
- BR | tell them, and walk out for good if they will not accept it | v: self-direction | mark: came out | chance: 60
- RG | cry it out at the kitchen table, and ask for a hug | v: benevolence, security | mark: came out | chance: 75
- UG | ask a cousin who already knows to sit in when you tell them | v: security, benevolence | mark: came out | chance: 65
- WB | keep it from them for good, and keep the family as it is | v: security, conformity | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 75
- WU | write them a long, careful letter, and give them a week to take it in | v: benevolence, self-direction | mark: came out | chance: 65
works: {parent} is quiet for a long moment, then reaches across the table and takes {Ns} hand.
works: It is not easy, and the questions go on for months, but the family holds, and {N} is part of it as {N} really is.
fails: {parent} leaves the room without a word, and the door stays closed for a long time.
fails: There are shouts and tears, and {N} leaves that night not knowing when the way back will open.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday pair 3 (juvenile to mature): calls WUG (cycle s4, attr >= 2) / calls BR (cycle s1, unease)

# gate: attr >= 2 & ~mark('came out') & age >= 16
== everyone asks when you will settle down
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 16-60
calls: WUG
tone: trouble
life: love, family
horizon: months
roles: parent, grandparent, sibling, prospect
earth: everyone asks when you will find someone of the other sex and settle down, and a match is being lined up for you
times: Most gay and bisexual people not out to their family, at gatherings through the twenties and thirties (estimate); Pew 2013: about 4 in 10 LGB adults had not told their mother
gap: 2-4
scene: At {sibling}'s wedding three aunts in a row ask {N} when it will be {Ns} turn, and {parent} has already seated a nice young person of the other sex next to {N} at dinner.
scene W: Everyone in the family was married by twenty-five. {N} can feel the expectation like a hand on the back, gentle and firm.
scene U: {N} counts it up: four relatives, two set-ups, one dating app {parent} installed without asking. The pressure is not going away; only the answer to it can change.
scene B: A good match would end the questions, please the family and open certain doors. {N} weighs that against a whole life, coldly.
scene R: {N} wants to stand on a chair and tell the whole hall to mind its own business.
scene G: {grandparent} talks of great-grandchildren as if they were already planted. {N} loves the old roots, and cannot grow the way they expect.
- W | go on the date your parents arranged, to keep the peace | self_control: - | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: gave in to pressure | chance: 85
- U | invent a demanding career as the reason, and keep them guessing | v: security | mark: kept it hidden | closed: approval: lying to the family; backfire: the story is checked, and it does not hold | chance: 75
- B | agree a marriage of convenience with a friend who has the same secret | binds | v: security, power | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 60
- R | say plainly, in front of everyone, that you will marry when and whom you please | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- G | lean on the cousin who never married, who seems to understand without asking | v: security (belonging), benevolence | chance: 75
- W>G | promise your grandparent you will think about it, to give them peace | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 70
- U>W | enrol on a long course far from home, so the questions have to wait | binds | v: self-direction, conformity | mark: moved away | chance: 80
- B>U | string the matchmakers along with vague answers, to learn who is pushing and why | v: security | chance: 95
- R>B | throw yourself into work and friends, and let the life you build answer them | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 70
- G>R | take on a plot at the allotments, and spend every weekend there | binds | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 80
works: The questions die down for a while, and {N} breathes more easily.
works: {N} gets through the season on {Ns} own terms, more or less.
fails: The pressure only grows, and every family gathering becomes a test.
fails: {parent} takes it badly, and the months at home turn cold.

# gate: unease & age >= 12
== asking to be called by another name
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 12-70
calls: BR
tone: mixed
life: identity, friends
horizon: moment
roles: friend, colleague, a teacher
earth: for the first time you ask someone to call you by the name that fits, and to use the words that fit
times: Most transgender and nonbinary people, once, most often in the teens or twenties; about 0.5 in 100 US adults and 1.4 in 100 of 13 to 17 year-olds identify as transgender (Williams Institute 2022)
once: yes
scene: {N} has written the name a hundred times on scraps of paper and deleted it from a hundred messages. Today, walking with {friend}, {N} finally asks {friend} to try saying it out loud.
scene W: {N} wants to ask properly, without fuss, the way one asks a favour that matters. Saying it feels like signing something.
scene U: {N} has chosen the name with care: how it sounds, what it means, how it looks written down. Now comes the part no amount of thinking can settle.
scene B: {N} has decided whom to ask first: the one person who will not laugh, and who can be trusted to keep it until {N} is ready.
scene R: {N} cannot bear to hear the old name one more time, and it comes out before {N} has quite decided: "Call me something else."
scene G: The new name fits like a coat that was always meant for {N}. Saying it aloud is only letting it be worn.
- W | ask your friend, politely and plainly, to use the new name from now on | v: self-direction | mark: named their gender | chance: 80
- U | write a short note that explains the name and the words, and hand it over | v: self-direction | mark: named their gender | chance: 80
- B | use the name first among strangers in another town, where nothing gets back home | door | v: security, self-direction | chance: 85
- R | introduce yourself by the new name to the next person you meet | identity | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: named their gender | chance: 85
- G | let the friends who know you best try the name, a little at a time | v: benevolence, security | mark: named their gender | act: let the friends who know them best try the name, a little at a time | chance: 80
- W>U | ask a teacher or a manager how such requests are handled, before saying anything | v: security | chance: 80
- U>B | change the name on your accounts first, so it is in place before anyone can argue | identity | v: self-direction, power | mark: named their gender | closed: approval: changing school or work records without asking; backfire: changed back, and a meeting called | norm: transition | chance: 80
- B>R | tell people the old name was a family nickname you have outgrown | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- R>G | go out with your friends that weekend as yourself, in clothes that fit the name | door | v: self-direction, hedonism | mark: named their gender | chance: 75
- G>W | wait until the family is ready, and keep the old name at home for now | v: security, benevolence | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 80
works: {friend} says the name, a little shyly, and {N} feels it land exactly where it belongs.
works: By the end of the month the name is simply what {N} is called.
fails: {friend} laughs, then sees {Ns} face and stops, but the moment is spoiled.
fails: The old name keeps coming back, and every slip stings.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday pair 4 (no gate; juvenile to elder): calls UG (cycle s4) / calls WBR (cycle s3)

== a friend comes out to you
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 13-95
calls: UG
tone: mixed
life: friends
horizon: moment
roles: friend
earth: a close friend tells you, nervously, that they are gay, and you are among the first to know
times: About half of people, once or twice in a life, most often in the teens and twenties (estimate); Pew 2013: nearly 9 in 10 Americans know someone gay or lesbian
gap: 20-40
share: .5
scene: Over a late coffee {friend} goes quiet, turns the cup round twice, and then says it in a rush, before the nerve goes: {friend} is gay, and {N} is the first person {friend} has ever told.
scene W: {N} was brought up on firm ideas of right and wrong, and some of them are speaking up right now. But {friend} is still {friend}, the same as five minutes ago, and waiting.
scene U: A dozen small things suddenly make sense to {N}: the stories that never had names in them, the subject changed so often. Questions crowd in.
scene B: {N} understands that {friend} has just handed over something precious and dangerous. Whoever holds a secret like this holds a little power, and {N} feels the weight of it.
scene R: {Ns} first impulse is to hug {friend} so hard the coffee spills. The second is to ask why on earth {friend} waited so long.
scene G: {N} looks at {friend}, the same face as at ten and at fifteen, and something settles: people are what they are, like weather, like rivers.
- W | say gently that it goes against what you were taught, but you still care | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- U | ask gentle questions about how long they have known and what it is like | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 85
- B | say it is fine, then quietly keep a little more distance from now on | v: security | mark: refused someone in need | chance: 88
- R | hug them hard, and say the friendship is exactly the same as before | v: benevolence | chance: 80
- G | carry on just as before, and let the friendship speak for itself | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 82
- W>G | promise to keep the secret and stand by them, whatever anyone says | binds | v: benevolence | mark: kept your word | chance: 80
- U>W | find out where the nearest support group meets, and offer to go along the first time | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 75
- B>U | tell one mutual friend in confidence, to see what they make of it | v: self-direction (curiosity) | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: outing a friend; backfire: it spreads, and the friend learns who started it | self_control: - | chance: 72
- R>B | say straight out that it makes you uncomfortable | v: security | act: say straight out that it makes them uncomfortable | chance: 80
- G>R | take them for a long walk in the hills, and let them talk it all out | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 78
works: {friend} lets out a long breath, and the evening goes on, lighter than before.
works: Something new and steady settles between {N} and {friend}.
fails: {friend} goes quiet, pays for the coffee and leaves early.
fails: Over the next weeks {N} and {friend} see less and less of each other.

== a pride march comes down the high street
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 12-100
calls: WBR
tone: joy
life: community
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, a neighbour
earth: a pride march with music and banners comes down the main street on a Saturday, and the whole town has an opinion
times: Many people in towns and cities, a few times in a life when a march comes through their own streets; others never (estimate)
gap: 10-20
share: .4
scene: On Saturday the high street fills with music, banners and a float with a brass band, and the march moves slowly past the shops. Some people on the pavement cheer, some film it, some turn their backs.
scene W: {N} notices the police lining the route, the permit taped to a lamppost, the church group with its own placards on the far corner. Everyone has a side, and the town is watching who stands where.
scene U: {N} watches faces as much as floats: the old couple holding hands at the front, the teenager trying not to cry, the man outside the bakery who will not look.
scene B: The street is packed, the shops are busy and the cameras are everywhere. {N} sees a chance to be seen there, or a reason not to be.
scene R: The drums hit {N} in the chest, the music is loud and the sun is out. Standing still is impossible.
scene G: The march is full of families, pushchairs and grandparents with flags. It looks less like a protest than a village fete that has learned to walk.
- W | stand on the pavement with the neighbours and clap the marchers past | identity | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 90
- U | ask a marcher what the march is for, and listen to the whole answer | door | v: universalism | chance: 85
- B | keep well away from the march, so nobody sees you there | v: security | act: keep well away from the march, so nobody sees them there | chance: 95
- R | jump in and dance with the marchers all the way to the park | body: light | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 95
- G | bring the family down with a picnic, as if it were the town fete | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 90
- W>R | volunteer as a steward for next year's march, keeping the route safe | v: benevolence, universalism | title: activist in a cause | chance: 70
- U>G | read up on the town's own history of people who had to hide | v: universalism | mark: learned a skill | chance: 75
- B>W | sell cold drinks along the route, and give a cut to the organisers | v: achievement, benevolence | chance: 65
- R>U | go and argue with the counter-protesters, to hear their reasons | door | v: self-direction, universalism | closed: approval: a shouting match in the street; backfire: the police step in | chance: 65
- G>B | turn your back on the march with the old families on the corner | identity | v: tradition, power | chance: 70
works: It turns into one of those days the town talks about for years, and {N} was there.
works: {N} goes home sunburnt and hoarse, with plenty to think about.
fails: A scuffle on the corner sours the afternoon, and {N} goes home early.
fails: Someone {N} knows saw {N} there, and has something to say about it on Monday.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- inner: a late realisation inside a marriage (cycle s4)

# gate: attr >= 2 & ~mark('came out') & age >= 35
== a late truth inside a marriage
tier: inner
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-85
holds: kind:partner
tone: mixed
life: inner, love, home
horizon: years
roles: partner, friend, child
requires: drawn to both sexes or mostly the same sex (attr >= 2), never came out (~mark('came out')), aged 35 or more and holding a partner (most late realisations come inside a long marriage); often after years of 'kept it hidden' or 'gave in to pressure'
likelier: a friendship with someone of the same sex that grows close; the children growing up and leaving; a death or an illness that makes time feel short; an era more open than the one they grew up in; women more often than men (attraction shifts more over the life course in women, Diamond 2008)
rarer: a community or a country that punishes it; a faith the whole family shares (it is felt the same, but pressed down for longer)
earth: in midlife, inside a long marriage, you admit at last that you are drawn to your own sex, and perhaps always were
times: Rare: a minority of gay and bisexual people married to the other sex, once, in midlife (estimate); women's attractions shift more over life than men's (Diamond 2008)
once: yes
scene: At a wedding {N} dances once with an old friend of the same sex and feels the jolt from school, the one {N} folded away and married past. Twenty years with {partner}, and it is still there.
scene W: {N} made vows and meant them. Now {N} lies awake beside {partner}, knowing those vows were made by someone who had not yet told the truth, even inwardly.
scene U: {N} looks back over the years and sees it everywhere, like a watermark held up to the light: the crushes explained away, the films watched twice, the unease at weddings.
scene B: {N} has built a good life: the house, the name, the friends. Every piece of it rests on a story {N} no longer believes, and {N} starts counting what the truth would cost.
scene R: Half a life lived at half volume. {N} feels it all at once, grief and relief and a fierce urge to do something about it now.
scene G: {N} feels like a tree that has grown round a wire for decades. The wire is still there; the tree has simply kept growing.
- W | keep faith with the marriage, and carry the truth quietly | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 85
- U | see a counsellor alone, and work out what is true before saying anything | self_control: + | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B | keep the marriage for the home you share, and lead a private life on the side | v: hedonism, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: deceiving a partner; backfire: found out, and the marriage and the trust break together | chance: 65
- R | tell your partner tonight, all of it, whatever happens next | self_control: + | v: self-direction, benevolence | mark: came out | chance: 60
- G | let it be what it is: a part of you, as it always was | v: self-direction (accepting oneself) | act: let it be what it is: a part of them, as it always was | chance: 60
- W>G | tell your partner honestly, and ask to stay a family whatever comes | v: benevolence | mark: came out | chance: 60
- U>W | plan a fair way to part, with a counsellor's help, before saying a word | v: universalism, security | chance: 65
- B>U | go to a support group in another town, under a different name | door | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- R>B | leave the marriage and start again on your own terms, at once | v: self-direction, hedonism | mark: left home | chance: 85
- G>R | go away alone to the sea for a week, without saying why | v: self-direction, hedonism | closed: approval: leaving home for a week with no reason given; backfire: the family fears the worst, and the trust frays | chance: 85
works: {N} finds words for it at last, and the ground holds.
works: Whatever comes next, {N} has stopped lying, at least inwardly.
fails: The truth comes out sideways and at the worst moment, and the house goes cold.
fails: {N} folds it away again, and the old weight settles back on {Ns} chest.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- inner: gender unease found late (cycle s2)

# gate: unease & ~mark('named their gender') & age >= 30
== a name that has waited for decades
tier: inner
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-85
tone: mixed
life: inner, identity, body
horizon: years
roles: friend, sibling
requires: gender unease (unease: trans or nonbinary), never named their gender (~mark('named their gender')), aged 30 or more; a turning point that leaves room to think (the children grown, a parent's death, a health scare, retirement) or a long quiet stretch
likelier: an era more open than the one they grew up in; reading about or meeting someone who changed their life late; the children grown; a parent's death
rarer: a job, a faith or a country that would punish it (it is felt the same, but said later or never)
earth: in midlife you admit at last that the sex you were raised as has never felt like your own, or not only your own, and that it is not going away
times: Rare: some transgender and nonbinary people who never came out, once, often after the children leave, a loss or retirement (estimate); about 0.5 in 100 US adults identify as transgender (Williams 2022)
once: yes
scene: Clearing a box in the loft, {N} finds an old school photo and stares at the child in it, dressed and named as everyone decided. Decades on, the feeling that child had has never once gone away.
scene W: {N} has done everything expected for decades: the work, the family, the part {N} was given. The duty was real. So is this, and {N} cannot carry both forever.
scene U: {N} has read about people who changed their lives at fifty and sixty, and kept every article in a folder. Tonight {N} takes the folder out again.
scene B: {N} has a career, a reputation and a lot to lose. {N} also has one life, half spent, and starts counting what the rest of it is worth.
scene R: {N} catches a reflection in a shop window and feels the old wrongness flare, sharp as the first time. Enough waiting.
scene G: Some things take a long season to rise. {N} feels this one rising now, slow and certain as sap in spring.
- W | go on as before, for the sake of the people who rely on you | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept it hidden | act: go on as before, for the sake of the people who rely on them | chance: 60
- U | talk it through with a doctor or a counsellor who knows this field | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- B | begin living as yourself in private, on your own terms, for now | v: self-direction | mark: kept it hidden | chance: 85
- R | tell the people closest to you this week, and start living as yourself | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: named their gender | act: tell the people closest to them this week, and start living as themselves | chance: 70
- G | accept the feeling as simply part of who you are, and let it come in time | v: self-direction (accepting oneself) | chance: 70
- W>B | find out the legal steps for a change of name, and get the papers ready | v: self-direction, security | closed: law: where the law allows no change of name or sex on papers; backfire: refused, and the request on record | law: gender on papers | chance: 90
- U>R | make a careful plan for the year you will start living openly | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- B>G | quietly find a group in the next city of people who have been through it | v: security (belonging) | chance: 65
- R>W | tell your partner or oldest friend everything, because they deserve the truth | v: benevolence | mark: named their gender | chance: 80
- G>U | go back to the town you grew up in, and walk it, remembering how it felt | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 80
works: For the first time in years, {N} feels that the life ahead might actually fit.
works: The decision settles, and {N} sleeps through the night.
fails: The fear wins for now, and {N} puts the folder back in the drawer.
fails: {N} says too much too soon to the wrong person, and the weeks that follow are hard.
